---
/**
 * Layer 2 of three: everything that styles a rendered Post body.
 *
 * Markdown arrives through a `<slot />` as already-rendered HTML with no
 * component of its own, so Astro cannot add its scope attribute to any of it.
 * Prose styling therefore CANNOT be scoped in the ordinary sense — but it can
 * be *housed*, and that is what this component is for. Two things follow:
 *
 * 1. It is testable. `Prose.test.ts` renders slotted content through the
 *    Container API and asserts the wrapper it lands in — the first coverage
 *    article prose has ever had.
 * 2. The list rules stop being scoped by a prefix someone has to remember.
 *    `.prose :global(ul)` compiles to `.prose[data-astro-cid-…] ul`: the
 *    narrowing comes from a scope attribute Astro generates, not from an
 *    `article ` someone typed. `.post-list` sits outside this element, so a
 *    prose list rule cannot reach it however specific it becomes. That is the
 *    guarantee the old comment could only ask for.
 *
 * `:global()` on the descendant, never on the whole selector. Wrapping the
 * `.prose` end would opt the rule out of scoping entirely and put us back
 * where we started.
 */
---

<div class="prose"><slot /></div>

<style>
  /* Markdown-native TL;DR: an ordinary heading immediately followed by a list.
     This works unchanged in .md and .mdx. Its styling is part of the frozen
     non-media style. */
  .prose :global(h2#tldr),
  .prose :global(h2#tldr + ul) {
    background: var(--chip);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
  }

  .prose :global(h2#tldr) {
    margin: 2.25rem 0 0;
    padding: 0.9rem 1.25rem 0.25rem;
    border-radius: 4px 4px 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 650;
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }

  .prose :global(h2#tldr + ul) {
    margin: 0 0 2.25rem;
    padding: 0.25rem 1.25rem 1rem 2.75rem;
    border-radius: 0 0 4px 4px;
  }

  .prose :global(h2#tldr + ul li + li) {
    margin-top: 0.35rem;
  }

  /* Prose list rules live here and nowhere else.
     A bare `ul { padding-left }` reaches every list on the site — the post
     list, and any nav or footer list added later — and indents rows meant to
     sit flush. Housing them in this component makes that structurally
     impossible rather than merely unlikely: the compiled selector carries this
     component's scope attribute, and `.post-list` is never inside it.
     e2e/post-list-alignment.spec.ts asserts the rows line up; the unit test in
     src/lib/prose-scope.test.ts asserts no list rule anywhere in the codebase
     goes unscoped. */
  .prose :global(ul),
  .prose :global(ol) {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }

  .prose :global(li + li) {
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
  }

  .prose :global(blockquote) {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    padding: 0.25rem 0 0.25rem 1.25rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--hairline);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .prose :global(hr) {
    border: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    margin: 2.5rem 0;
  }

  /* Plain Markdown images: centered, with room to breathe. Direct children of
     the body or of a paragraph ONLY — an image inside a component (an
     ArticleImage figure, a MediaAside, a YouTube poster) is that component's
     to place, and this rule must not reach it.
     cursor: zoom-in signals the Lightbox component's click-to-enlarge
     affordance (progressive enhancement — harmless with JS disabled). */
  .prose > :global(img),
  .prose :global(p) > :global(img) {
    display: block;
    margin: var(--space-figure) auto;
    cursor: zoom-in;
  }

  /* ── The Markdown tier of the figure contract (#119) ──
     41 plain images across 35 Posts, framed with no author edits and no syntax
     to remember. The only hook a Markdown image offers is the element itself,
     so the rules are keyed on the PATH — which is not a trick, it is the only
     door: Astro's native Markdown processor has no remark/rehype hooks, and
     adding one would not add a plugin, it would migrate the whole pipeline
     onto unified. Refused twice before, refused again here.

     The corpus already follows conventions, so the path is enough:

       *.excalidraw.svg   sketch  — inverted on a dark ground
       other .svg         drawn   — no plate, no dimming; it is already ink
       raster             one framed treatment, dimmed on a dark ground

     Photo versus screenshot is NOT inferred from a filename. Too fragile for
     the gain, and one raster treatment serves both.

     THE LIMIT, stated rather than glossed: this is FRAMING, not the figure.
     These images never become a `<figure>`, never carry a `data-media` kind
     and never take a caption — that is the component tier's, and it is why
     the contract is described as two-tier. Breakout is likewise a component
     gesture and is deliberately not offered here; that discharges ADR 0006 §4
     with a refusal rather than a mechanism.

     Astro rewrites a processed raster to `/_astro/<name>.<hash>.webp` and
     leaves an SVG as `.svg`, keeping the original basename in both — which is
     what makes `[src*='excalidraw']` and `[src$='.svg']` reliable. */

  /* Raster. The same treatment the `photo` kind gets in src/styles/media.css,
     because "equivalent framing" has to mean equivalent — asserted in
     src/lib/figure.test.ts so the two tiers cannot drift apart. */
  .prose > :global(img:not([src$='.svg'])),
  .prose :global(p) > :global(img:not([src$='.svg'])) {
    border-radius: 8px;
  }

  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :global(:root:not([data-theme='light'])) .prose > :global(img:not([src$='.svg'])),
    :global(:root:not([data-theme='light']))
      .prose
      :global(p)
      > :global(img:not([src$='.svg'])) {
      filter: brightness(0.92);
    }
  }

  :global(:root[data-theme='dark']) .prose > :global(img:not([src$='.svg'])),
  :global(:root[data-theme='dark']) .prose :global(p) > :global(img:not([src$='.svg'])) {
    filter: brightness(0.92);
  }

  /* Sketch. Excalidraw exports dark strokes on a transparent background —
     legible on a white page, invisible on a dark one. Invert lightness and spin
     the hue back so black-on-transparent reads as light-on-transparent, without
     maintaining two exported files.

     This rule moved here from the global sheet in #119, narrowed from "any
     sketch on the page" to "a sketch in a Post body", which is the only place
     one has ever appeared. It also OVERRIDES the raster dimming above by
     source order — a sketch is not a photograph and must not be dimmed as
     well as inverted.

     THE TRAP: `prefers-color-scheme` asks the OPERATING SYSTEM and cannot see
     a theme the reader forced here. On a light OS with dark forced, the page
     goes dark via color-scheme and this rule never fires — dark strokes on a
     dark ground, invisible. So the query is narrowed to "OS dark AND not
     forced light", and a forced-dark companion sits outside it.
     src/lib/theme.test.ts asserts both halves are present, in this file. */
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :global(:root:not([data-theme='light'])) .prose :global(img[src*='excalidraw']) {
      filter: invert(0.92) hue-rotate(180deg);
    }
  }

  :global(:root[data-theme='dark']) .prose :global(img[src*='excalidraw']) {
    filter: invert(0.92) hue-rotate(180deg);
  }

  /* ── Code ── */

  .prose :global(code) {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
  }

  .prose :global(:not(pre) > code) {
    font-size: 0.875em;
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: var(--chip);
  }

  .prose :global(pre) {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    border-radius: 8px;
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  /* ── Tables ── */

  .prose :global(table) {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .prose :global(th) {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .prose :global(th),
  .prose :global(td) {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    /* `anywhere`, not `break-word`. Only `anywhere` reduces a cell's MIN-CONTENT
       width, which is what an auto-layout table uses to decide it cannot fit —
       `break-word` wraps the rendered text but leaves the intrinsic width alone,
       so the table still forces the page wider. Without this a table with more
       columns than the viewport can hold pushes the whole PAGE sideways: `pre`
       has scrolled inside its own box since it was written, and `table` never
       got the equivalent. Found by the kitchen-sink fixture at 320px.
       This upholds the existing no-horizontal-scroll guarantee rather than
       changing the frozen style — column widths on a desktop are unaffected,
       because the constraint only ever binds when the table cannot otherwise
       fit. */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>
